## Q1) What security practices do you follow in Node.js?

### Answer
Never trust client input. Protect secrets, use HTTPS, harden headers, rate-limit sensitive routes, and keep dependencies updated.

### Checklist

| Practice | What to do | Why |
| :------- | :--------- | :-- |
| Input validation | Validate/sanitize body, query, params | Stops injection & bad payloads |
| Auth | JWT or `HttpOnly` cookies + bcrypt/argon2 | Protects accounts |
| Secrets | Env vars / secret manager, never commit `.env` | Prevents credential leaks |
| HTTPS / TLS | Force HTTPS in production | Encrypts traffic |
| Helmet | Secure HTTP headers | Helps against XSS/clickjacking |
| Rate limiting | Limit login/API abuse | Stops brute force / DoS |
| CORS | Allow only trusted origins | Blocks unwanted browsers calls |
| Dependencies | `npm audit`, update packages | Avoid known CVEs |
| Least privilege | Minimal DB/OS permissions | Limits damage if hacked |
| Errors | No stack traces to clients in prod | Hides internals |
| Uploads | Check type/size; store safely | Prevents malware/RCE |
| Dangerous APIs | Avoid `eval`, unsafe `child_process` | Prevents code injection |

### Request security flow

```text
Client
  -> HTTPS
  -> Helmet + CORS + Rate limit
  -> Auth
  -> Validate input
  -> App + DB (least privilege)
  -> Safe response (no secrets)
```

### One-liner
> Validate input, hide secrets, HTTPS + Helmet, rate-limit auth, patch dependencies.

