## Q2) What is libuv in Node.js?

### Answer
**libuv** is a C library used by Node for the **event loop** and **async I/O**.  
JavaScript runs on one main thread; libuv handles waiting on I/O efficiently.

### What libuv does

| Part | Role | Examples |
| :--- | :--- | :------- |
| Event loop | Schedules callbacks when work is ready | timers, I/O callbacks, `setImmediate` |
| Async network I/O | Non-blocking sockets via OS APIs | TCP/HTTP (epoll/kqueue/IOCP) |
| Thread pool | Offloads some blocking work | `fs`, `dns.lookup`, some `crypto`/`zlib` |
| Timers / signals | OS-level async primitives | `setTimeout` support under the hood |

### Important correction (interview favorite)
- **Network I/O** → usually non-blocking OS APIs (not thread pool)
- **File system / dns.lookup / some crypto** → often **thread pool** (default size 4)
- JS callbacks still run on the **main thread**

### Diagram

```text
Your JS code
     |
     v
   V8 (runs JavaScript)
     |
     v
   libuv
    / \
   /   \
Event   Thread Pool
Loop    (fs, dns.lookup, crypto...)
   |
   +-- Network I/O (non-blocking OS)
   |
   v
Callback queued -> runs on main thread
```

### One-liner
> libuv powers Node's event loop and async I/O; some blocking tasks use its thread pool, then callbacks return to the main thread.

