## Q3) Explain Node.js architecture.

### Answer
Node is a **single-threaded, event-driven** runtime:  
**V8** executes JS, **libuv** handles async I/O, and **Node APIs** (`fs`, `http`, etc.) expose that to your app.

### Layers

```text
+----------------------------------+
| Your App (Express/Nest/JS)       |
+----------------------------------+
| Node.js APIs (http, fs, path...) |
+----------------------------------+
| V8 Engine (execute JS)           |
+----------------------------------+
| libuv (event loop + async I/O)   |
+----------------------------------+
| Operating System                 |
+----------------------------------+
```

### Request flow

```text
1. Request comes in
2. Node starts async I/O via libuv (non-blocking)
3. Main thread keeps serving other work
4. I/O finishes -> callback queued
5. Event loop runs callback
6. Response sent
```

### Key traits

| Trait | Meaning |
| :---- | :------ |
| Single-threaded JS | One main call stack for JS |
| Event-driven | Work continues via callbacks/promises |
| Non-blocking I/O | Don't idle waiting for disk/network |
| Best for | APIs, realtime chat, I/O-heavy apps |
| Weak for heavy CPU | Use `worker_threads` or another service |

### One-liner
> Node = V8 + libuv + Node APIs; one JS thread handles many connections through non-blocking I/O and the event loop.

